<?xml version="1.0"?>
<ScenContainer xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">
  <ScenTitle>Wargasm, 1962 V4</ScenTitle>
  <ScenDescription>&lt;BODY scroll=auto&gt;&lt;P align=left&gt;&lt;FONT color=red&gt;Scenario&amp;nbsp;4.0 updated for CMO v 1.03 Build 114.25&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;SAC delivers thermonuclear war to the USSR - August 1962&lt;?xml:namespace prefix = "o" ns = "urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" /&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;As&amp;nbsp;Commander in Chief, President John F. Kennedy was&amp;nbsp;surprised to discover that the Joint Chief's of Staff (JCS) master nuclear war plan, SIOP-62 (Single Integrated Operational Plan for fiscal year 1962) required a massive&amp;nbsp;attack&amp;nbsp;against targets in the USSR, Peoples Republic of China (PRC) and Democratic Peoples Republic of Korea (DPRK) regardless of whether the latter countries were involved in the triggering crisis or not.&amp;nbsp; Everything nuclear in the US arsenal was to be launched or carried to well over a thousand targets in a massive, single and prolonged&amp;nbsp;attack dubbed by some unknown wag as a "wargasm".&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;The plan&amp;nbsp;provided no real options between not attacking at all and expending&amp;nbsp;every available nuclear weapon in one hugely destructive strike.&amp;nbsp; That&amp;nbsp;SIOP-62 was a significant improvement over what came before says much about the chaos that surrounded American nuclear war planning in the 1950's.&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;The&amp;nbsp;principle&amp;nbsp;elements of SIOP-62 were deemed to be sound&amp;nbsp;so Strategic Air Command (SAC), the JCS, the President's National Security Council (NSC) and other agencies went to work on SIOP-63; preparing at least five-additional optional responses and the&amp;nbsp;specialized target lists they required.&amp;nbsp; By 1962 over 400 civilian and more than 600 major military&amp;nbsp;sites had been identified as probable high priority targets in the USSR alone and&amp;nbsp;the total number of potential targets&amp;nbsp;numbered into the thousands.&amp;nbsp; Many had more than one aim point and multiple weapons assigned to ensure&amp;nbsp;complete destruction (usually with a generous overkill allowance) and SAC planned to deliver in excess of 3500 megatons (MT) to targets in the Soviet Union without using the Navy's Polaris submarines.&amp;nbsp; Even today most of the specifics of the various historical SIOPs&amp;nbsp;and associated target lists&amp;nbsp;are classified but those for SIOP-62 may be found online.&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;You are Commanding Officer, 92nd Strategic Aerospace Wing, a&amp;nbsp;SAC formation based at Fairchild Air Force Base near Spokane WA.&amp;nbsp; DEFCON-1 has just been ordered and nuclear release is authorized by the national command authority.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;The causes of this sudden escalation to nuclear war&amp;nbsp;are irrelevant&amp;nbsp;to the execution of your orders so no narrative is included.&amp;nbsp; You are to&amp;nbsp;implement the war plan in accordance with the orders found in the scenario briefing and target list provided.&amp;nbsp; Only your piece of the operation is simulated, other SAC wings are assumed to be conducting their own operations under centralized command and control from SAC HQ.&amp;nbsp; A non-editable Navigation Zone prevents entry of your aircraft into airspace that&amp;nbsp;has been&amp;nbsp;allocated to other wings for operations or that is otherwise restricted.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; At this time roughly one-third of SAC's bombers and all serviceable ICBM's were designated on alert, that is to say ready to launch in less than 15-minutes.&amp;nbsp; For game purposes you have&amp;nbsp;to&amp;nbsp;select targets for your Wing, a target list is provided as a printable *.rtf file&amp;nbsp;and the rationale for this is discussed in the designer's notes.&amp;nbsp;You have&amp;nbsp;the complete SAC wing, 45 x B-52 bombers, 36 x KC-135 tankers and nine Atlas-E missiles.&amp;nbsp; Facilities at Elmendorf AFB in Alaska&amp;nbsp;are available to you and&amp;nbsp;there is&amp;nbsp;a handful of assets there that you can draw upon.&amp;nbsp; Targets and known defences are&amp;nbsp;visible on the map and you may discover others and even targets of opportunity as the operation unfolds.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;EM&gt;"The bomber will always get through."&amp;nbsp; - &lt;/EM&gt;Stanley Baldwin, British PM - 1936&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;&lt;EM&gt;"Nothing new about death, nothing new about deaths caused militarily..."&amp;nbsp; - &lt;/EM&gt;General Curtis LeMay, Commander SAC -&amp;nbsp;1948-57&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;Scenario Designer's Rambling Notes:&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;&amp;nbsp;A previous CMANO scenario &lt;EM&gt;Deter Detect, Defend&lt;/EM&gt; looked at the problem of air defence of Pacific North America against a Soviet bomber threat as it was feared to exist in 1962.&amp;nbsp;This scenario takes place concurrently and examines&amp;nbsp;a similar&amp;nbsp;situation from the perspective of a nearby SAC Wing Commander.&amp;nbsp; The initial&amp;nbsp;idea for both scenarios was born&amp;nbsp;while doing research into the Cold War nuclear arms race for a non-gaming project and grew out&amp;nbsp;a&amp;nbsp;thought regarding the strategic nuclear alert forces, their commanders and crews if faced with the worst-case and yet seriously anticipated situation.&amp;nbsp; In other words:&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;What might it have been like to come back from lunch to find DEFCON-1 sitting&amp;nbsp;on top of&amp;nbsp;your in-basket?&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;The&amp;nbsp;difference between scenario concepts is that&amp;nbsp;in this situation&amp;nbsp;the Player gets to attack with forces that were actually in existence and on alert in 1962 and the OOB for the 92nd Strategic Aerospace Wing should be reasonably accurate for August of that year.&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;Reading about nuclear war planning during the Cold War can be a surreal experience, particularly if you&amp;nbsp;grew up in&amp;nbsp;it, served in any branch of the military (thinking any NATO country here) and accepted uncritically the&amp;nbsp;propaganda and continual threat assessments as delivered regularly by your leadership.&amp;nbsp; I suspect that we tended to&amp;nbsp;believe what we were told regarding the "enemy" because our leaders generally believed that what they were telling us was an accurate and capital T "Truth" and so they carried the sincerity of&amp;nbsp;conviction.&amp;nbsp; But hindsight is 20/20 and one can reach&amp;nbsp;different conclusions today regarding the what the actual threat level was at the time given the massive amounts of information&amp;nbsp;available since the collapse of the Soviet Union and the declassification of at least some relevant US documents.&amp;nbsp; That said, it's amazing that intelligent, well-educated, articulate, thoughtful and war-experienced men (and planning for nuclear war was an almost exclusively male undertaking) could contemplate in all seriousness&amp;nbsp;a situation where the&amp;nbsp;deaths of 70-million Americans and the destruction of some one-third of infrastructure in the CONUS&amp;nbsp;represented some sort of victory provided SAC and the USN managed to inflict around 130-million Soviet dead and that three-fifths of the property in the USSR could &amp;nbsp;be&amp;nbsp; destroyed. Such numbers were apperently part of an official casualty assessment by the NSC.&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;Director Stanley Kubrick after making the 1964 film &lt;EM&gt;Dr. Strangelove or How I Learned to Stop Worrying and Love the Bomb&lt;/EM&gt; supposedly remarked "It's doubtful that most civilizations survive their nuclear age." although we seem to have gotten through the worst of it, at least so far.&amp;nbsp; Deterrence worked, in part because regardless of how irrational the situation might have appeared to the outsider, inside there were dedicated professionals ready to turn the keys and fly the bombers that they probably&amp;nbsp;believed would destroy everything they valued.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;Policy makers&amp;nbsp;knew and understood&amp;nbsp;this so&amp;nbsp;few direct provocations were offered, brinkmanship was minimized and dialog generally substituted for confrontation.&amp;nbsp; The people at the top were always aware that their subordinates would really turn their keys and drop their bombs (or die trying) so that those very orders would never be given.&amp;nbsp; This scenario presumes that, for whatever reasons deterrence has failed and&amp;nbsp;a full-scale nuclear war option has been selected.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;There is a fair bit of scenario author's licence&amp;nbsp;in &lt;EM&gt;Wargasm 1962&lt;/EM&gt;&amp;nbsp;since there is no reason to think that&amp;nbsp;any SAC&amp;nbsp;Wing Commander would actually be responsible for directly allocating his assets to&amp;nbsp;targets in the manner allowed using CMANO but more on this below.&amp;nbsp; Although the SIOP's and associated target lists&amp;nbsp;remain classified, there is data in the public domain&amp;nbsp;that allows some reasonable speculation as to the form that may have been used at the planning level.&amp;nbsp; I have elected to provide the Player with a target list that features the yield to be delivered (in MT) and perhaps some information about the possible defenses that may be encountered but no restrictions are imposed on the Player regarding target selection.&amp;nbsp; Targets are a mix of military and civilian; in the jargon of the day counterforce and countervalue respectfully.&amp;nbsp; What nuclear weapons do best is destroy urban centres, something that the Soviet Union's leadership&amp;nbsp;may&amp;nbsp;have understood better than the US war-planners.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;Evidence suggests that the Russians had largely rejected extensive counterforce targeting in the CONUS, opting intead to target conventional military bases (to prevent reinforcement of Europe) and urban centres.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;SIOP-62 was superceded in July 1962 by&amp;nbsp;the new plan; SIOP-63, which offered&amp;nbsp;new&amp;nbsp;options for limiting attacks based on circumstances and clarified target priorities leaning towards&amp;nbsp;a counter-force doctrine.&amp;nbsp; In addition the requirement to automatically attack the PRC and DPRK was dropped&amp;nbsp;presumably with seperate target lists to cover those countries.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;Cities as such were largely omitted and in their place as 2nd priority targets was substituted "Command and Control Centres", the majority of which just happened to be located in urban areas.&amp;nbsp; This probably allowed the planners to play&amp;nbsp;a semantic game&amp;nbsp;where civilians were not actually targets for nuclear attack although multi-megaton missiles and bombs lacked the precision to discriminate between&amp;nbsp;military and non-military infrastructure.&amp;nbsp; In this scenario we will assume that the Player&amp;nbsp;controls the targeting priorities.&amp;nbsp; The intention is that this&amp;nbsp;should make for better replay value but without any evidence as to what the targets were for the 92nd SAW in 1962, imposing&amp;nbsp;a specific plan on the Player seemed unsatisfactory from a game play standpoint.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;In reality, the attack plans were very likely closely scripted with cruise and attack altitudes, ingress and egress routes, tanker boxes, alternate targets, RV's&amp;nbsp;and IP's all spelt out in great detail.&amp;nbsp; Targets were prioritized and&amp;nbsp;individual weapons were alloted to specific aim points, which then became&amp;nbsp; Designated Ground Zeros (DGZ).&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;Planners had done all of this in advance so when the orders were taken from their safes it became a matter of execution and allowing for current weather conditions rather than additional target planning.&amp;nbsp; The scenario deliberately has none of this, there are no&amp;nbsp;missions for the Player&amp;nbsp;and all detailed planning and coordination within the Wing are a Player responsibility.&amp;nbsp; Unrealistically as well, no time on targets (TOT) are&amp;nbsp;specified but one can probably assume with a high degree of confidence that TOT's would have been&amp;nbsp;built into the SIOP.&amp;nbsp; Here you have the flexibility to stage your strikes anywhere within the 24-hours of game time but a warning: wait too long and some unpleasent things might start happening in the CONUS.&amp;nbsp; Even if Wing Operations did produce the detailed individual&amp;nbsp;plans no doubt they would have required the blessings of SAC HQ as the organization itself was highly centralized but&amp;nbsp;in any event, the detailed plans would have been prepared in advance of&amp;nbsp;the Wing assuming alert status.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;It is recommended that before actually starting the scenario and while the game is paused, spend as long as it takes examining the available intelligence, planning your sorties and creating the required missions.&amp;nbsp; Many of the air bases in the USSR&amp;nbsp;were found using Google Earth but&amp;nbsp;finding&amp;nbsp;reliable data for&amp;nbsp;the air defences in Soviet Amur and Maritime oblasts&amp;nbsp;during&amp;nbsp;the early 1960's&amp;nbsp;proved impossible so the scheme&amp;nbsp;in the&amp;nbsp;scenario&amp;nbsp;and the USSR order of battle&amp;nbsp;is speculative.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;&lt;EM&gt;"If we again have such a victory I fear we shall be ruined."&lt;/EM&gt; - King Pyrrhus, 279 BCE&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;Thank you&amp;nbsp;for trying &lt;EM&gt;Wargasm 1962&lt;/EM&gt;.&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/BODY&gt;</ScenDescription>
  <Complexity>4</Complexity>
  <Difficulty>4</Difficulty>
